WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:10.123
Today, kids in foster care – even those who suffer&nbsp;
from behavioral and mental health challenges&nbsp;&nbsp;– are not

00:00:10.123 --> 00:00:16.160
very likely to be placed in a group care&nbsp;
setting. Of the 425,000 children in foster care,

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:21.863
only 55,000 are in group homes, treatment&nbsp;
facilities, and other congregate care settings – a big

00:00:21.863 --> 00:00:25.600
drop since the early&nbsp;2000s. 
  What's behind this decline?

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:30.640
In the middle of the 20th century, philanthropists&nbsp;
responded to reports of orphanages as uncaring,

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:36.720
greedy operations, with a focused effort to keep&nbsp;
more kids at home or with foster families. Federal

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:41.840
and state governments joined in this effort, but&nbsp;
today, it's clear this movement has gone too far.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:46.800
Despite the good intentions of philanthropists,&nbsp;
struggling foster kids now have few places to go.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:52.800
Last summer alone, more than 400 children were&nbsp;
sleeping in offices in Texas. Philanthropists

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:58.609
should focus on building more quality congregate&nbsp;
care facilities like Utah Youth Village's Teaching-Family

00:00:58.609 --> 00:01:02.914
Model – an evidence-based program where&nbsp;
married couples help kids develop the skills

00:01:02.914 --> 00:01:08.594
they need to live in a real home. Programs like&nbsp;
this are the standard for the future of group care.

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:14.000
Do you think that there should be more quality&nbsp;
congregate care facilities for foster kids?

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:18.080
Let us know in our poll. Also, let us know&nbsp;
what other topics you'd like our scholars

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:24.400
to cover in 60 seconds, and be sure to like and&nbsp;
subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

